This directory contains various Python tools that work on the ACL2 books in 
support of extracting actionable data from the books for training machine
learning models. The most important file is convert_acl2data.py, which is the
entry point into the postprocess phase.

ALL programs in this directory are in the public domain, under the same license
as ACL2, i.e., based on the BSD-3-Clause.

* checkpoint_to_clause.py -- attempts to convert an ACL2 checkpoint (as printed 
	to the user) into clausal form as used for machine learning.

* collect_defpkg.py -- finds definitions of packages in ACL2 community books, and
	allows you to lookup a symbol and see which packages it is defined in. This is
	a command-line utility. Run with --help for details.

* collect_runes.py -- finds definitions of many rule names (RUNEs) in ACL2 
	community books, and allows you to lookup a rune and see in what file it is
	defined. Run with --help for details.

* convert_acl2data.py -- finds *.acl2data.out files (produced by the "gather" 
	phase) in a directory and translates each such file into a .mli (Machine 
	Learning Input) file. The MLI file format is specified in the Kestrel PEARLS
	project documentation.

	Usage:
	  python convert_acl2data.py [OPTIONS] <input>
	
	Arguments:
	  <input>               .tgz file, directory, or individual .out file
	
	Options:
	  -j, --workers N        Number of parallel workers (default: nproc).
	                          Use 1 for serial processing.
	  --no-incremental       Force reprocessing even if .mli already exists.
	                          By default, existing .mli files are skipped.
	  --log-level LEVEL      Logging level: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR
	                          (default: DEBUG)
	
	Examples:
	  # Process a .tgz archive (uses all cores, skips existing):
	  python convert_acl2data.py /path/to/acl2data.tgz
	
	  # Process with 4 workers, force reprocessing, less output:
	  python convert_acl2data.py -j 4 --no-incremental --log-level WARNING \\
	       /path/to/acl2data.tgz
	
	Output:
	  For a .tgz input, .mli files are written alongside the .tgz preserving
	  the tar's internal directory structure.  For a directory input, .mli
	  files are written next to each .out file.  Each .mli file is a JSON
	  array of records with this structure:
	
	  {
	    "metadata": {
	      "file": "[books]/path/to/book.lisp",
	      "rune": "EVENT-NAME",
	      "rule-classes": "(:REWRITE)",
	      "goal": <translated goal term>,
	      "goal-str": "string form of goal",
	      "broken-goal": <translated broken goal>,
	      "broken-goal-str": "string form of broken goal"
	    },
	    "input": {
	      "checkpoint-type": "top" | "induction",
	      "checkpoint": ["clause1", "clause2", ...],
	      "checkpoint-acl2": <untranslated checkpoint>,
	      "checkpoint-structure": <parsed checkpoint with renamed vars>,
	      "checkpoint-sequence": ["(", "NOT", "NATP", "var-0", ")", ...],
	      "symbol-map": {"original-var": "var-0", ...}
	    },
	    "output": {
	      "action": "human-readable description",
	      "action-type": "use-lemma" | "add-hyp" | "add-hint" |
	                      "add-use-hint" | "add-by-hint" |
	                      "add-enable-hint" | "add-disable-hint" |
	                      "add-expand-hint" | "add-do-not-hint" |
	                      "add-nonlinearp-hint" | "add-induct-hint" |
	                      "add-cases-hint" | "add-library" |
	                      "advice <type>",
	      "action-obj": "the specific lemma/hint/rune/hypothesis string"
	    }
	  }
	
	Bug fixes and enhancements (branch fix-acl2data-converter, 2026-06-30):
	  - Added missing "import tarfile" (was NameError).
	  - Fixed tarfile member.name access (TarInfo objects are not strings).
	  - Decoded tarfile content from bytes to latin-1.
	  - Skip :HASH and :EXPANSION-STACK sections silently (were ERROR logs).
	  - Handle :HINT-SETTING-ALIST entries with 6 or 7 elements (was crash).
	  - Moved regex compilations to module level in parse_acl2.py (was
	    recompiling on every recursive call, causing stack pressure).
	  - Changed .tgz handler to write .mli files incrementally per-member
	    instead of accumulating everything in memory (was OOM killed).
	  - Preserved tar directory structure in output .mli paths.
	  - Added ProcessPoolExecutor for parallel .tgz processing (-j flag).
	  - Added incremental mode (skip existing .mli files, --no-incremental
	    to override).
	  - Added argparse-based CLI (--help for usage).

* fix_defpkg.py -- finds duplicate/conflicting package information in the ACL2
	community books. This depends on the package database produced with
	collect_defpkg.py. Run with --help for details.

* ld_instr.py -- (very) poor man's "gather" process. Executes an ACL2 file and
	collects information about the hints and lemmas used to feed a very simple
	machine learning model. Strictly a prototyping tool.

* learn_enable.py -- uses the data gathered by ld_instr.py to train a machine
	learning model in enabling runes. Strictly a prototyping tool.
	
	This is an early prototype that trains a scikit-learn DecisionTreeClassifier
	on .summ files (produced by ld_instr.py).  It predicts whether a rune
	should be enabled or disabled based on function symbols present in the
	theorem form.  This is a simpler approach than training on the full .mli
	checkpoint structure.

* parse_acl2.py -- Python parser for ACL2 S-expressions.
	
	Bug fix (2026-06-30): moved regex compilations (FRACTION_REGEX,
	DECIMAL_REGEX) to module level.  Previously re.compile() was called
	inside the recursive get_sexpr() function, recompiling on every call.

* pkg_consts.py -- definition of common package constants used in defpkg commands.

* slurp_runes.py -- collects rule names in an ACL2 file

* summarize_mli.py -- collects basic information about the data in .mli files.
	Counts occurrences of each (action-type, action-obj) pair across all
	.mli files in a directory tree.
	
	Usage:
	  python summarize_mli.py [OPTIONS] <dir1> [dir2 ...]
	
	Options:
	  -j, --workers N        Number of parallel workers (default: nproc).
	  --log-level LEVEL      Logging level (default: DEBUG)
	
	Example:
	  python summarize_mli.py --log-level INFO \\
	       /workspaces/acl2-jupyter/data/books
	
	Output: per action-type, total count and top-5 most frequent action-obj.
	
	Bug fixes and enhancements (2026-06-30):
	  - Fixed TypeError on unhashable list action-obj (e.g., (:INSTANCE ...)).
	  - Added parallel processing with ProcessPoolExecutor (-j flag).
	  - Switched from recursive directory walk to os.walk.
	  - Added argparse CLI (--help for usage).


============================================
Machine Learning Pipeline Overview
============================================

The overall ML pipeline has three phases:

Phase 1: GATHER (Lisp side, already done)
  - ACL2 is patched to record "retry experiments" when proofs fail.
  - For each failing theorem, ACL2 tries removing hypotheses, hint
    settings, book runes, and individual runes one at a time.
  - Results (checkpoints, success/failure) are written to
    *__acl2data.out files.
  - These are collected into acl2data.tgz (1.4GB compressed, 5692 files).

Phase 2: POSTPROCESS (Python side — this directory)
  - convert_acl2data.py: .out → .mli JSON
    Each record maps a checkpoint (the failing proof state) to an action
    (the lemma/hint/rune that must be added back to fix the proof).
    Variables are normalized to "var-0", "var-1", etc.
    Output: ~5692 .mli files alongside the .tgz.
  
  - summarize_mli.py: aggregate statistics
    Counts which action-types and lemmas appear most frequently.
    Helps understand the dataset distribution.

Phase 3: TRAINING (not yet implemented)
  The training/ directory (referenced in the top-level README) was
  planned but not yet built.  The .mli format supports training models
  that predict what fix to apply given a checkpoint.

  Training approaches to explore:
  
  a) Token-based classifier (simpler, faster):
     - Tokenize the checkpoint-sequence
     - Train a classifier (XGBoost, Random Forest, etc.) to predict
       (action-type, action-obj) pairs
     - Input features: checkpoint tokens + goal-str tokens + action-type
     - This is what learn_enable.py prototyped at a smaller scale
  
  b) Tree-LSTM / Transformer (more powerful):
     - Encode the S-expression structure of the checkpoint
     - Captures term structure and variable relationships
     - Requires more data and compute
  
  c) Graph Neural Network:
     - Represent the checkpoint as a term DAG with shared sub-expressions
     - Most expressive, but most complex
  
  Integration with ACL2:
     - When a proof fails, extract the checkpoint
     - Query the trained model: "what action fixes this?"
     - Apply top-N predicted hints and retry
     - Use active learning to improve over time
